{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git show f5ce7146 -- docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md 2>/dev/null; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '155,225p;320,360p;1950,1980p;2150,2205p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | head -85; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '140,185p'\"","aggregated_output":"commit f5ce714694a1bd4910fef3a40e9392b064e53a59\nAuthor: Sonny To <son.c.to@gmail.com>\nDate:   Thu Oct 1 23:48:59 2026 +0700\n\n    docs(design): retire the B6 predecessor doc; fold its load-bearing content into the linker spec\n    \n    The completion audit (collab/1790871424000) confirmed all five\n    milestones satisfied, meeting the owner's deferral condition. The B6\n    predecessor's caveats are folded in place (the D14 proof distinction,\n    the B6 ordering rationale, the index re-minting rule), dead citations\n    re-pointed, the section 9 M1 instruction amended to record the merge\n    and deletion, and the two over-long spec lines reflowed (criterion 4\n    now clean).\n\ndiff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\nindex f7985f13..d0220ede 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md\n@@ -1036,7 +1036,7 @@ before R2 or R4.\n \n     Fulfilled by: Phase B6 (src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc,\n     test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc)\n-    Specification: docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md\n+    Specification: docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\n     Must not change: stack H, dao.stream, dao.jing, dao.jing.dht\n \n Per the owner's directive (\"B6 should be used by both the stack and register\n@@ -1046,7 +1046,7 @@ are fulfilled directly within the unified B6 linker phase. Phase B6 delivers\n function, the register format record (`:yin.debruijn.register`), the R index,\n and same-root pairing datoms (`[root :yin.debruijn.register/hash R]`) with\n both trusted and verifying fallback paths. Full specification, file box,\n-and completion criteria are defined in `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`.\n+and completion criteria are defined in `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md`.\n \n ## 7. Non-goals and protected surfaces\n \ndiff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md\nindex 52bf8516..5d66c9e3 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md\n@@ -716,14 +716,15 @@ lanes must agree under the normalizer.\n     Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, dao.stream,\n     dao.jing, dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, named VM semantics\n \n-B6 is specified in `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, the\n+B6 was specified in `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, the\n standalone linker design for Phase B6, used by both the stack and register\n-VM for linking code over `dao.stream`. That document specifies the format-\n-parameterized fetch function over `dao.stream` / `dao.jing`, the address and\n-identity distinction, the six-step fetch protocol, the qualified refusal\n-vocabulary, the H and R indexes, the transitional content-hash risk, and the\n-completion list. Section 7.2 below remains the topology summary and D8, D9,\n-D11, D13, D14, D15, and D16 remain the governing decisions.\n+VM for linking code over `dao.stream`; that document was merged into\n+`docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md` and deleted (2026-10-01), which now\n+specifies the format-parameterized fetch function over `dao.stream` /\n+`dao.jing`, the address and identity distinction, the six-step fetch\n+protocol, the qualified refusal vocabulary, the H and R indexes, and\n+the completion list. Section 7.2 below remains the topology summary and\n+D8, D9, D11, D13, D14, D15, and D16 remain the governing decisions.\n \n ### B7: dependency closure linker\n \ndiff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\nindex 77cf9d95..843b51ab 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\n@@ -1,10 +1,15 @@\n # yin.vm.linker: the universal code linker over dao.stream\n \n Status: design, proposed (2026-09-24, revision r11). Supersedes the\n-namespace placement and the two-backend scope of\n-`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` (Phase B6); that document's fetch pipeline,\n-format records, refusal vocabulary, and same-root pairing are carried\n-here in substance and extended to all four `yin.vm` execution backends.\n+namespace placement and the two-backend scope of the Phase B6\n+predecessor `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`; that document's fetch\n+pipeline, format records, refusal vocabulary, and same-root pairing\n+are carried here in substance and extended to all four `yin.vm`\n+execution backends. Its remaining load-bearing content was merged\n+into this document and the predecessor file was deleted (2026-10-01),\n+on the completion audit's confirmation that all five milestones of\n+section 9 landed:\n+collab/1790871424000-qa-yin-vm-linker-spec-completion-audit.md\n Revision r2 reconciled the adversarial review\n `collab/1790250614225-reviewer-universal-linker-spec.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md`;\n revisions r3 to r11 reconcile the round-2 to round-10 consensus\n@@ -18,8 +23,6 @@ sections 4 and 5, and the ledger of `yin.vm.ledger`.\n \n Related documents:\n \n-- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` - the B6 predecessor (two\n-  formats, one synchronous fetch)\n - `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` - governing decisions D5 to D16\n - `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` - AST rows, segment vectors,\n   derivation records\n@@ -167,9 +170,12 @@ it and is never the interface another interpreter is written against.\n run.** The storage address and the format identity have different\n preimages for two of the four formats and the same preimage for the other\n two; in every case both checks execute, because an index entry is a claim\n-and never a proof (`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` section 6; D14). Every\n-check is directed by the algorithm the address or identity carries; the\n-linker never re-mints under a default and compares with `=` (Class 3 of\n+and never a proof: step 2 proves the retrieved value is the content at\n+that storage address, step 3 proves that content is the exact program\n+requested, and neither check subsumes the other (D14,\n+`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`). Every check is directed by the algorithm\n+the address or identity carries; the linker never re-mints under a\n+default and compares with `=` (Class 3 of\n `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`).\n \n **I6. The VM never invokes a loader.** Absence of code is a park plus a\n@@ -201,7 +207,9 @@ defect B6 guarded against with D9, and the unification here adds a third.\n \n **Storage address.** `(jing/segment-key payload)` under the storage\n encoder in force. Changes when the encoder changes (the CBOR landing,\n-`dao.jing.md` *Open items*). Carries its own hash algorithm.\n+`dao.jing.md` *Open items*); when it does, every identity-to-address\n+index is re-minted, while the contract-pinned identities (H and R) and\n+the code they name are unchanged. Carries its own hash algorithm.\n \n **Format identity.** What the VM contract calls the code. There are two\n families:\n@@ -331,10 +339,15 @@ is one more record.\n \n ### 4.2 The six steps\n \n-The order is B6's, unchanged, and remains load-bearing for the reasons\n-B6 gives. An admission check precedes step 1; step 2 is a bounded\n-worklist; steps 1 and 2 are stream exchanges (section 6); step 5 has a\n-linker half and a receiver half.\n+The order is B6's, unchanged, and remains load-bearing: the address\n+check (step 2) precedes the identity check (step 3) because the\n+address was requested from the store; validation (step 4) precedes\n+the scans of step 5 so the scanners only ever see structurally valid\n+values; and the closure check (step 5) precedes return so an unclosed\n+image is never handed to the caller (D11). An admission check\n+precedes step 1; step 2 is a bounded worklist; steps 1 and 2 are\n+stream exchanges (section 6); step 5 has a linker half and a receiver\n+half.\n \n ```text\n (link runtime format identity)\n@@ -788,8 +801,9 @@ operational: a \"local\" require and a \"remote\" require are the same four\n appends and four reads.\n \n The content pair is `dao.jing.content`'s request and response vocabulary\n-(`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1): `{:jing/request r :jing/get address}`\n-answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`, over local\n+(`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1):\n+`{:jing/request r :jing/get address}` answered by\n+`{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`, over local\n ring buffers or reflections of remote streams, with the shared ingress\n check `dao.jing/accept-bytes!` applied to every answer. The linker writes\n no protocol of its own for content.\n@@ -1747,7 +1761,8 @@ as a pure function over plain data.\n   datom, carrying its entity's proof value (an envelope with no\n   proof datom, or whose entity carries two distinct proof values, is the\n   no-proof case below; a proof datom with no envelope datom names no\n-  event, and joins one only if an envelope later appears on its entity), with the carrier\n+  event, and joins one only if an envelope later appears on its\n+  entity), with the carrier\n   `:dao.stream/identity` of the read supplied by the composition, never\n   read from a datom. An envelope is honored only with\n   a proof; a bare `:asserted-by` value is a string anyone can write.\n@@ -1948,9 +1963,14 @@ flowchart TD\n test likewise. Update the five design documents that name the old\n namespace (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`,\n `dao.agent.md`, `dao.agent.harness.md`, `dao.agent.mcp.server.md`) and\n-the orchestrator log. Mark `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` as superseded by\n-this document in its status line. No behavior changes. The B6 test\n-suite passes unchanged apart from the require alias.\n+the orchestrator log. The predecessor document\n+`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` was first marked superseded by this\n+document in its status line (2026-09-25); when the completion audit\n+(collab/1790871424000-qa-yin-vm-linker-spec-completion-audit.md)\n+confirmed all five milestones landed, its remaining load-bearing\n+content was merged into this document and the file was deleted\n+(2026-10-01). No behavior changes. The B6 test suite passes unchanged\n+apart from the require alias.\n \n **M2. Four format records.** Replace `:hash-fn` with `:identity-fn` and\n `:identity-matches-fn`; rename `:free-names-fn` to `:obligations-fn`;\n@@ -2148,7 +2168,8 @@ Structure:\n \n Verification:\n \n-5. Every B6 completion criterion (section 11 there) holds for all four\n+5. Every B6 completion criterion -- the refusal matrix, the structure\n+   rules, and the cross-host transfer rules -- holds for all four\n    formats where applicable, through both `fetch` and `step`.\n 6. A payload whose address is valid but whose identity is not the one\n    requested is refused for every format, including the two whose\n   155\tcodec unchanged: no function, handle, or ambient state (`dao.stream.md`,\n   156\t*Envelopes*).\n   157\t\n   158\t**I3. Location is not identity.** Whether the content is in this process,\n   159\tin a file, behind a socket, or on a DHT peer changes how bytes are\n   160\tobtained, not what the linker checks (`dao.jing.md`, *Reads*). A local\n   161\tcomposition and a remote one differ in the creation specification of the\n   162\tstreams they wire, and in nothing the linker can observe.\n   163\t\n   164\t**I4. No operation waits.** The portable linker interface is a step that\n   165\treturns what is true now, including \"not yet\" (`dao.stream.md`, OD-5). A\n   166\twaiting `fetch` may wrap the step as host policy on hosts that can afford\n   167\tit and is never the interface another interpreter is written against.\n   168\t\n   169\t**I5. Verification is address-directed and identity-directed, and both\n   170\trun.** The storage address and the format identity have different\n   171\tpreimages for two of the four formats and the same preimage for the other\n   172\ttwo; in every case both checks execute, because an index entry is a claim\n   173\tand never a proof: step 2 proves the retrieved value is the content at\n   174\tthat storage address, step 3 proves that content is the exact program\n   175\trequested, and neither check subsumes the other (D14,\n   176\t`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`). Every check is directed by the algorithm\n   177\tthe address or identity carries; the linker never re-mints under a\n   178\tdefault and compares with `=` (Class 3 of\n   179\t`dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`).\n   180\t\n   181\t**I6. The VM never invokes a loader.** Absence of code is a park plus a\n   182\trequest emission; the loaded image arrives as a value in VM state\n   183\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 7.2). The linker is an observer on a\n   184\trequest stream, not a function the kernel calls.\n   185\t\n   186\t**I7. The registry is a value.** `yin.vm.module` already keeps the\n   187\tregistry as a map in VM state. This document keeps that and changes what\n   188\tthe map holds: manifests, derivation records, portable slices, and\n   189\tper-task bindings lowered from them, produced by an evaluation the\n   190\tscheduler ran, never code that arrived on a stream.\n   191\t\n   192\t**I8. Fail closed.** Every ambiguity the linker can detect (two manifests\n   193\tfor one name, an image whose derivation does not lead back to the\n   194\tmanifest's tree, an obligation whose profile differs) is a refusal, never\n   195\ta choice.\n   196\t\n   197\t## 3. Identities: three kinds, one address space\n   198\t\n   199\tThe linker moves between three kinds of name. Confusing them was the\n   200\tdefect B6 guarded against with D9, and the unification here adds a third.\n   201\t\n   202\t| Kind             | Example                              | Owner            |\n   203\t|------------------|--------------------------------------|------------------|\n   204\t| storage address  | `:segment/blake3-...`                | `dao.jing`       |\n   205\t| format identity  | H, R, a vector address, a root row id| the format       |\n   206\t| module name      | `'my.lib`                            | name environment |\n   207\t\n   208\t**Storage address.** `(jing/segment-key payload)` under the storage\n   209\tencoder in force. Changes when the encoder changes (the CBOR landing,\n   210\t`dao.jing.md` *Open items*); when it does, every identity-to-address\n   211\tindex is re-minted, while the contract-pinned identities (H and R) and\n   212\tthe code they name are unchanged. Carries its own hash algorithm.\n   213\t\n   214\t**Format identity.** What the VM contract calls the code. There are two\n   215\tfamilies:\n   216\t\n   217\t- *Contract-pinned identities* (H, R): computed by the format's own hash\n   218\t  function over a descriptor that embeds the lowering contract version,\n   219\t  pinned to SHA-256 by contract freeze, independent of the storage\n   220\t  encoder. Their preimage differs from the storage address's preimage.\n   221\t  For these two formats, storage address and VM identity are strictly\n   222\t  decoupled (D9).\n   223\t- *Storage-derived identities* (`:yin.ast/code`, `:yin.semantic/code`):\n   224\t  UCF section 7.3.2 defines a segment's `:yin.code/hash` as the\n   225\t  `segment-key` of its canonical instruction vector, and\n   320\t  scanner is the application-row query over the same relation. For\n   321\t  the vector formats all three are operand scans by pc (`:var`\n   322\t  against `:store-put` and `:define`, `:load-free` against `:define`\n   323\t  and `:store-put`, the call opcodes for applications) with\n   324\t  conditional target ranges taken from the `:jump-if` and body\n   325\t  operands. Under Rule R (`yin/def` is syntax, never a name) a\n   326\t  definition is recognized by syntax alone: the tree's\n   327\t  `(yin/def <literal-symbol> value)` application and the vectors'\n   328\t  `:define` instruction. The definition operator is never an\n   329\t  obligation: every validator refuses it as a variable, binder, or\n   330\t  store key (`:reserved-name`), and the VM constructors refuse a\n   331\t  supplied env, store, or registry binding it. A format whose scanner\n   332\t  returns positions as `nil` is admitted and falls under the\n   333\t  conservative degradation of step 5a: every occurrence is retained\n   334\t  as an obligation, and with no application positions no occurrence\n   335\t  inside a lambda body is discharged at all.\n   336\t\n   337\t`fetch` and `step` contain no branch on `:format`. Adding a fifth backend\n   338\tis one more record.\n   339\t\n   340\t### 4.2 The six steps\n   341\t\n   342\tThe order is B6's, unchanged, and remains load-bearing: the address\n   343\tcheck (step 2) precedes the identity check (step 3) because the\n   344\taddress was requested from the store; validation (step 4) precedes\n   345\tthe scans of step 5 so the scanners only ever see structurally valid\n   346\tvalues; and the closure check (step 5) precedes return so an unclosed\n   347\timage is never handed to the caller (D11). An admission check\n   348\tprecedes step 1; step 2 is a bounded worklist; steps 1 and 2 are\n   349\tstream exchanges (section 6); step 5 has a linker half and a receiver\n   350\thalf.\n   351\t\n   352\t```text\n   353\t(link runtime format identity)\n   354\t\n   355\t0. admission     request malformed                  -> :invalid-request\n   356\t                 no format record for the format    -> :unsupported-format\n   357\t                 request contract differs from the record's\n   358\t                                                    -> :contract-mismatch\n   359\t1. address    <- (index identity)               linker-local index\n   360\t                 absent entry                       -> :absent\n  1950\t\n  1951\t```mermaid\n  1952\tflowchart TD\n  1953\t  M1[\"M1 rename\\nyin.vm.debruijn-linker -> yin.vm.linker\"]\n  1954\t  M2[\"M2 four format records\\nidentity-directed match, bounded parts\"]\n  1955\t  M3[\"M3 stepped core + link runtime\\nfetch = host policy; DHT behind server\"]\n  1956\t  M4[\"M4 require lowers to the linker\\nmanifests, authority, child install\"]\n  1957\t  M5[\"M5 yin.repl wires the four streams\\nper backend\"]\n  1958\t  M1 --> M2 --> M3 --> M4 --> M5\n  1959\t```\n  1960\t\n  1961\t**M1. Rename.** Move `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc` to\n  1962\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc` with namespace `yin.vm.linker`; move the\n  1963\ttest likewise. Update the five design documents that name the old\n  1964\tnamespace (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`,\n  1965\t`dao.agent.md`, `dao.agent.harness.md`, `dao.agent.mcp.server.md`) and\n  1966\tthe orchestrator log. The predecessor document\n  1967\t`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` was first marked superseded by this\n  1968\tdocument in its status line (2026-09-25); when the completion audit\n  1969\t(collab/1790871424000-qa-yin-vm-linker-spec-completion-audit.md)\n  1970\tconfirmed all five milestones landed, its remaining load-bearing\n  1971\tcontent was merged into this document and the file was deleted\n  1972\t(2026-10-01). No behavior changes. The B6 test suite passes unchanged\n  1973\tapart from the require alias.\n  1974\t\n  1975\t**M2. Four format records.** Replace `:hash-fn` with `:identity-fn` and\n  1976\t`:identity-matches-fn`; rename `:free-names-fn` to `:obligations-fn`;\n  1977\tadd `:contract`, `:row-defect-fn`, and `:parts-fn`; add `ast-format`,\n  1978\t`semantic-format`, `semantic-free-names`, and `row-local-defect`. Make\n  1979\tstep 2 the bounded worklist. `yin.vm.content` keeps `materialize-tree!`\n  1980\tand `materialize-vector!` as the mint side (they are `publish!` for those\n  2150\t`record-format`, the four free-name, definition, and application-site\n  2151\tscanners, `row-local-defect`, `index-from-datoms`, `address-attribute`,\n  2152\t`trusted-fallback`, `verifying-fallback`, `refused`, `refused?`, `ok?`,\n  2153\t`refusal-reasons`.\n  2154\t\n  2155\t## 11. Completion criteria\n  2156\t\n  2157\tStructure:\n  2158\t\n  2159\t1. One `fetch` and one `step` serve all four formats with no\n  2160\t   format-specific branching; a fifth format is one record.\n  2161\t2. No global loader, registry, callback, or cache; every input is an\n  2162\t   argument or VM-state value; no function or handle ever appears in a\n  2163\t   value on the link or content streams.\n  2164\t3. Tri-host parity (JVM, Node, ClojureDart), 0 kondo errors, clean\n  2165\t   cljstyle.\n  2166\t4. This file and `yin.vm.linker` are pure ASCII with every line at most\n  2167\t   80 columns.\n  2168\t\n  2169\tVerification:\n  2170\t\n  2171\t5. Every B6 completion criterion -- the refusal matrix, the structure\n  2172\t   rules, and the cross-host transfer rules -- holds for all four\n  2173\t   formats where applicable, through both `fetch` and `step`.\n  2174\t6. A payload whose address is valid but whose identity is not the one\n  2175\t   requested is refused for every format, including the two whose\n  2176\t   identity is a storage address, and under every registered algorithm.\n  2177\t7. A request naming a contract the format record does not implement is\n  2178\t   `:contract-mismatch` before validation runs.\n  2179\t8. A malformed row is refused before any of its children is requested,\n  2180\t   and every composition bound produces `:parts-limit`.\n  2181\t9. Local `fetch` produces exactly the content-pair traffic the stepped\n  2182\t   path produces and holds no content handle.\n  2183\t\n  2184\tRequire:\n  2185\t\n  2186\t10. `(require 'foo)` on each of the four backends, over ring buffers,\n  2187\t    parks, links, installs through the child phases, and resumes with\n  2188\t    `'foo`; the same over the JVM WebSocket content path.\n  2189\t11. The scheduler registry holds no function that arrived by linking\n  2190\t    and no lowered bindings, only the portable slice; every task-local\n  2191\t    `:bindings` was lowered from a slice an isolated child evaluation\n  2192\t    produced, which reached halt with every export bound.\n  2193\t12. UCF section 7.4.1 carries `:link-request`, `:link-response`, and\n  2194\t    `:install` safepoints, section 7.4.3 their pending variants, section\n  2195\t    7.5.1 the `:yin.k/binding` closure variants, the `:yin.k/store-of`\n  2196\t    key, and the re-sealed references of section 7.3, sections 7.5.1,\n  2197\t    7.5.3, and 7.6.2 the private engine-resource table of section\n  2198\t    7.3 and the resource decode targets and store model it moves\n  2199\t    out of the store, and a parked entry of each kind lifts and\n  2200\t    lowers through UCF's round-trip test.\n  2201\t13. Every swapped-image and swapped-name case in M4 refuses before load.\n  2202\t14. The assertion policy of section 8.2 refuses `:ambiguous-name`,\n  2203\t    discards unproven assertions as `:unauthenticated`, and ignores\n  2204\t    undeclared principals, at a fixed snapshot.\n  2205\t15. A module that defines a name and then reads it in the main\n     1\t# yin.vm.linker over dao.jing.dht: publish, load and evaluate code by name\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **contract frozen 2026-10-01 (linker-over-DHT epic slice L-design,\n     4\trevision r5); slices L0 to L5 are implemented and landed on master\n     5\t(df7cf1f4).** Revision r5 closes the one finding\n     6\tof the r4 sign-off\n     7\t(<code>collab/1790808100000-architect-linker-over-dht-design-doc-signoff<!--\n     8\t-->-r4.gpt-6-sol.findings.md</code>):\n     9\t`:republished` reports any result change after the first report, whatever\n    10\twrote the ledger (5.5.3). Revision r4 closed the two\n    11\tfindings of the r3 sign-off\n    12\t(<code>collab/1790808100000-architect-linker-over-dht-design-doc-signoff<!--\n    13\t-->-r3.gpt-6-sol.findings.md</code>):\n    14\tthe repair queue has its own capacity and its own admission turn, and\n    15\tpublications awaiting a first report are bounded (5.5.2, 5.5.4). Revision\n    16\tr3 closed the two\n    17\tlifecycle findings of the r2 sign-off\n    18\t(<code>collab/1790808100000-architect-linker-over-dht-design-doc-signoff<!--\n    19\t-->-r2.gpt-6-sol.findings.md</code>)\n    20\tand adopts owner decision 5, automatic retry while open: section 5.5 is\n    21\trewritten around one complete publication ledger. Revision r2 closed the five\n    22\tfindings\n    23\tof the withheld sign-off\n    24\t(<code>collab/1790808100000-architect-linker-over-dht-design-doc-signoff<!--\n    25\t-->.gpt-6-sol.findings.md</code>):\n    26\tthe bounded replicate backlog and the publication result (5.5), dependency\n    27\tnames (7.4), the `:unaskable` and walk-defect shapes (4.3, 9), and the\n    28\tfootprint computation (5.2). Section 2 records the starting tree. Section 12\n    29\tis the slice plan, L0 to L5. Subordinate to\n    30\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md),\n    31\t[`yin.vm.linker.md`](./yin.vm.linker.md),\n    32\t[`dao.jing.dht.md`](./dao.jing.dht.md),\n    33\t[`yin.repl.link-policy.md`](./yin.repl.link-policy.md) and\n    34\t[`yin.repl.dao.space-index.md`](./yin.repl.dao.space-index.md). In sections\n    35\t3 to 11 every sentence is a rule.\n    36\t\n    37\tInputs: the lead design\n    38\t(`collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`),\n    39\tthe second opinion\n    40\t(<code>collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht-second-opinion.gpt<!--\n    41\t-->-6-sol.findings.md</code>,\n    42\tAGREE-WITH-CHANGES, corrections adopted), and the owner decisions of\n    43\t2026-10-01\n    44\t(`collab/1790808000000-orchestrator-linker-over-dht-owner-decisions.md`).\n    45\t\n    46\t## 1. Overview and scope\n    47\t\n    48\tDHT epic S5 gave any Clojure program, and `yin.repl` through host functions,\n    49\tone path to join the DHT, load a published covered index from its manifest\n    50\taddress, and query it. Loading code *for evaluation* is a different path: the\n    51\tlinker resolves a module name to a module manifest address through a name\n    52\tenvironment, and links the manifest's image over a content pair. This\n    53\tdocument joins the two.\n    54\t\n    55\tIt answers one question: how does a second process, attached to the same\n    56\tDHT, load a module by name and evaluate it, with every trusted claim stated?\n    57\t\n    58\tOwner invariants this contract is bound by:\n    59\t\n    60\t- \"plain clojure code should be able to query for code in the dht. the\n    61\t  yin.repl should use the same path as the clojure repl via host-functions\"\n    62\t  (2026-10-01). Every operation here is a plain Clojure function;\n    63\t  `yin.repl` adapts effects to those functions and holds no second loader.\n    64\t- Code is shared over the stream linker; the linker boundary is\n    65\t  `dao.stream`; local versus remote linking is the composition's choice of\n    66\t  streams.\n    67\t- `dao.stream` is peer to peer: no server, no client, no privileged node.\n    68\t  Publishing and trusting are per-node composition, never network roles.\n    69\t- Derive, don't persist: nothing is stored that a query over existing rows\n    70\t  derives.\n    71\t- `dao.jing` is syntax and agents are semantics: the store stays passive and\n    72\t  payload-agnostic.\n    73\t- Code and continuations are datoms.\n    74\t\n    75\t**Out of scope:** latest-root discovery, key rotation and revocation, the\n    76\tpersistent stepped linker, the `:lease` link policy, authenticated node ids.\n    77\tSection 13 lists every deferral.\n    78\t\n    79\t## 2. The starting tree (master `c66809fa`)\n    80\t\n    81\t<table>\n    82\t<tr>\n    83\t<th>\n    84\tFact\n    85\t</th>\n   140\t  contract body; the `:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`, `next` and\n   141\t  `append!`; the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces; the OD-3 wording\n   142\t  corrected so `attach!`'s `not-found` and a stale cursor are separate\n   143\t  cases.\n   144\t- `dao.lease.md` (done): served entries and relay pairs named as lease\n   145\t  subjects; the carriage note names the `not-found` protocol error.\n   146\t- `dao.stream.ws.md` (done): the stream belongs to the peer that holds it;\n   147\t  the copy path versus attachment to the original; dialer-side and\n   148\t  acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption and RPC items point to the\n   149\t  spec.\n   150\t- `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (done): 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite\n   151\t  the spec; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's source outcome;\n   152\t  `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the acceptance\n   153\t  row cites slice 8 here.\n   154\t- `dao.jing.md` (done): the coordinate shape and its today/target split;\n   155\t  the deprecated backend bullet; `dao.jing/accept-bytes!` marked as the\n   156\t  target; the Base64 carriage named as channel-codec message boundaries.\n   157\t- `yin.vm.linker.md` (done): the content pair vocabulary cites section 1\n   158\t  here; the 6.1 table row, 6.3 shape, M3, deps and failure-policy timing.\n   159\t- `dao.data.btree.md` (done): the 5.4 backend row annotated as the target\n   160\t  name for `dao.jing.remote`; the Phase 4 landing note.\n   161\t- `dao.jing.dht.md` (done): the NAT limitation.\n   162\t- `daostream-udp-design.md` (done): superseded status line.\n   163\t- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (done): the framing claims corrected to channel-codec\n   164\t  message boundaries with Base64 in the application value.\n   165\t- `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, `dao.space.query.md`,\n   166\t  `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md`\n   167\t  (done): one status sentence each naming the successor.\n   168\t- `dao.jing.hash-registry.md`, `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`\n   169\t  (done): one-line pointers that the `dao.jing.remote` sites describe\n   170\t  today's code and the successor keeps the checks and classifications.\n   171\t- `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md` (done): the minimum viable stack's content\n   172\t  step marked historical, pointing at `dao.jing.content`.\n   173\t- Remaining, with the code: the `dao.stream.ws.md` residual dialer/acceptor\n   174\t  vocabulary beside its older deposit-model wording, and `dao.stream.md`'s\n   175\t  own Close-section \"server-side accepted-connection handle\", track the\n   176\t  contract's wording and are re-worded with the code slices, not before.\n   177\t\n   178\t## 5. Unification: the fate of every existing path\n   179\t\n   180\tMoved from the spec's section 8, which keeps only the normative summary.\n   181\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree, and its fate:\n   182\t\n   183\t- `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart adapters: **subsumed**.\n   184\t  Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium plus the spec's 3.1\n   185\t  projection. Its accept and disclaim frames and served-path table become\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
